<% content_for :title, "Spike or Stay: will this trend last?" %>
<% content_for :description, ApplicationHelper::DEFAULT_DESCRIPTION %>
<%= render "shared/nav" %>

<main class="paper">
  <%# ---------- Opening ---------- %>
  <header class="wrap opening">
    <p class="kicker">A Shopify app · v0 · backtested on <%= TrendModel.instance.trained_on["n"] %> past trends</p>
    <h1>Get an early read on whether a trend will stay.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Some products blow up overnight and are forgotten a month later. Others keep selling for years.
      Spike or Stay watches how many people read about each product on Wikipedia and, six weeks into a surge,
      makes a call: likely to last, or likely to fade.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <%= link_to "Open the dashboard", dashboard_path, class: "btn btn-primary" %>
      <%= link_to "Read the method", methodology_path, class: "btn btn-outline" %>
    </div>
  </header>

  <%# ---------- Fig. 1: two real trends ---------- %>
  <figure class="wrap fig">
    <div class="fig-panels">
      <% landing_trends.each do |trend| %>
        <% label, confidence, tone = landing_call(trend) %>
        <div class="fig-panel tone-<%= tone %>">
          <div class="fig-head">
            <strong><%= trend["name"] %></strong>
            <span class="label tone-<%= tone %>"><span class="label-dot"></span><%= label %> · <%= percent(confidence) %></span>
          </div>
          <%= landing_figure_svg(trend) %>
          <p class="fig-note">
            A year later: <%= trend["outcome"] == "sustained" ? "still" : "only" %>
            <span class="num"><%= percent(trend["late_mean"] / trend["peak"]) %></span> of its peak
            <%= trend["outcome"] == "sustained" ? "(lasted)" : "(faded)" %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      <strong>Fig. 1.</strong> Weekly Wikipedia views, 8 weeks before each surge to a year after.
      <span class="key key-window"></span> the six weeks the model reads before making its call;
      <span class="key key-threshold"></span> the bar for "lasted": months 6–12 must average a quarter of the early peak.
      Both trends were in the training data, so this shows how it works, not how accurate it is.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <%# ---------- The numbers (all real) ---------- %>
  <section class="wrap">
    <table class="facts">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">Backtest summary</caption>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Past trends backtested</th><td class="num stat-strip-n"><%= TrendModel.instance.trained_on["n"] %></td>
          <th scope="row">Model accuracy (leave-one-out)</th><td class="num"><%= BacktestStats.percent(:a, :logreg) %> <span>95% CI <%= BacktestStats.interval(:a, :logreg) %></span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">"Still rising" baseline</th><td class="num"><%= BacktestStats.percent(:a, :naive) %></td>
          <th scope="row">Lookahead</th><td class="num">0 weeks <span>enforced by tests</span></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <%# ---------- How it works ---------- %>
  <section class="wrap section" id="how">
    <h2 class="section-head-num"><span class="num">01</span> How it works</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><h3>Match</h3><p>Each product's type, or its title, is matched to a Wikipedia article. A sanity check catches bad matches, and you can fix one in a click.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Detect</h3><p>A surge starts when weekly views pass 3× their usual level and 2,000 views, two weeks running. One-day news blips don't count.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Call</h3><p>A logistic regression reads only the first six weeks after the surge and estimates the chance interest is still there six months on.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <%# ---------- What you see ---------- %>
  <section class="wrap section">
    <h2 class="section-head-num"><span class="num">02</span> What you see</h2>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="prose-block">
        <p>A card for every product: its label, a 26-week sparkline and the reason in plain words. Products that share an
          article (twelve snowboards, say) become one card. When there isn't enough data, it says so instead of guessing.</p>
        <p>Open a card for two years of weekly views, the bar the surge had to clear, and how each early signal pushed the
          model toward "spike" or "sustained". The pushes add up to the probability on the label.</p>
      </div>
      <%# Fig. 2: the air fryer replay's real contributions (Dec 31, 2017). %>
      <figure class="fig fig-small">
        <div class="explain-mock">
          <div class="em-row"><span>Peak sharpness</span><div class="em-bar"><i class="pos" style="width:46%"></i></div><b class="num">+0.77</b></div>
          <div class="em-row"><span>Volatility</span><div class="em-bar"><i class="pos" style="width:19%"></i></div><b class="num">+0.31</b></div>
          <div class="em-row"><span>Acceleration</span><div class="em-bar"><i class="neg" style="width:5%"></i></div><b class="num">−0.07</b></div>
          <div class="em-row"><span>Growth</span><div class="em-bar"><i class="neg" style="width:3%"></i></div><b class="num">−0.04</b></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption><strong>Fig. 2.</strong> How the model decided on the air fryer: a starting point of −0.25 plus these pushes gives a 67% chance it lasts.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# ---------- Replays ---------- %>
  <section class="wrap section" id="replays">
    <h2 class="section-head-num"><span class="num">03</span> Replays</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">Pick a past date and the app answers using only the data that existed then. A year later, it checks
      whether the call came true. (as_of = 2017-12-31 means only weeks ending on or before Dec 24, 2017 are used.)</p>
    <%# Calls and outcomes as the live replays show them (store demo). %>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data-table replays">
        <thead><tr><th>Replay date</th><th>Product</th><th>Call</th><th>A year later</th><th>In training data?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <% [["2017-06-12", "Fidget spinner", "Likely spike", "warning", "Faded", true],
              ["2017-12-31", "Air fryer", "Likely sustained", "success", "Lasted", true],
              ["2024-12-08", "Pop Mart", "Likely sustained", "success", "Lasted", false],
              ["2025-01-05", "Labubu", "Likely sustained", "success", "Lasted", true]].each do |date, product, call, tone, outcome, trained| %>
            <tr>
              <td class="num"><%= link_to human_date(Date.iso8601(date)), dashboard_path(as_of: date) %></td>
              <td><%= product %></td>
              <td><span class="label tone-<%= tone %>"><span class="label-dot"></span><%= call %></span></td>
              <td><%= outcome %> ✓</td>
              <td><%= trained ? "Yes, not a fair test" : "No" %></td>
            </tr>
          <% end %>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# ---------- Caveats ---------- %>
  <section class="wrap section">
    <h2 class="section-head-num"><span class="num">04</span> Caveats</h2>
    <ul class="caveats">
      <li><strong>Small sample.</strong> <%= TrendModel.instance.trained_on["n"] %> labelled trends. Against the "still rising" rule the model wins by
        <% model_k, = BacktestStats.score(:a, :logreg) %><% naive_k, = BacktestStats.score(:a, :naive) %><%= model_k - naive_k %> trends, which is within noise.</li>
      <li><strong>Written down first.</strong> Thresholds, features and analyses were committed before any model ran; two later changes are logged with reasons.</li>
      <li><strong>Interest isn't sales.</strong> Wikipedia readers aren't buyers. Labels describe public interest, not your demand.</li>
      <li><strong>Only abrupt surges.</strong> Slow, steady growth never trips the 3× rule, so it shows as "No surge detected".</li>
    </ul>
    <p><%= link_to "Full method and results →", methodology_path %></p>
  </section>

  <%# ---------- FAQ ---------- %>
  <section class="wrap section" id="faq">
    <h2 class="section-head-num"><span class="num">05</span> Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details><summary>Why Wikipedia pageviews?</summary><p>They're free, public, daily and go back to 2015, so the model can be backtested on real past trends. They measure interest, not sales: readers aren't buyers, and brand articles dilute product interest.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What counts as a "surge"?</summary><p>Weekly views above 3× the median of the previous 26 weeks and at least 2,000 views, for two weeks in a row. Slow, gradual growth never triggers this, so those products show "No surge detected".</p></details>
      <details><summary>How accurate is it?</summary><p>On <%= TrendModel.instance.trained_on["n"] %> past trends, <%= BacktestStats.percent(:a, :logreg) %> leave-one-out accuracy (95% CI <%= BacktestStats.interval(:a, :logreg) %>), versus <%= BacktestStats.percent(:a, :naive) %> for a simple "still rising in week 6" rule. A promising signal, but the gap is within noise at this sample size.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does it use my sales data?</summary><p>Not yet. It reads only product titles, types and tags. Tying labels to order data is the top next step.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What if a product maps to the wrong article?</summary><p>Every card has a "Fix mapping" form: point the product at the right article, or mark it "Don't track". On the public demo, your fixes only apply to you.</p></details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap closing">
    <p>See what's surging in a real catalog, or rewind to 2017.</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <%= link_to "Open the dashboard", dashboard_path, class: "btn btn-primary" %>
      <%= link_to "Air fryer replay", dashboard_path(as_of: "2017-12-31"), class: "btn btn-outline" %>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>

<%= render "shared/footer" %>
